Dissecting a capture in several processes
=========================================

This document describes why and how TShark can dissect one capture file in
several processes (the --jobs option), what it refuses to do and why, and
what it costs. For the mechanics of the planner see flow_shard.h; for the
other half of the large-capture problem see README.memory-spill.

1. The problem
--------------

Dissection is single threaded. A capture that takes a minute to analyze
takes a minute on a 10-core machine, and the only way to use the rest of
the machine is to split the file by hand - which splits flows, and so
changes the results.

2. Why processes
----------------

Dissection is not re-entrant and cannot be made so at a reasonable cost:

  - 574+ dissector source files carry mutable file-scope statics;
  - the wmem scopes are process globals (wmem_scopes.c still carries a
    "TODO: Make these thread-local");
  - the tap queue is a global array;
  - libgcrypt's initialisation is documented as not thread safe.

Auditing 3000 dissectors for re-entrancy is not a project anyone would
finish. fork() gives isolation for nothing, and forking after epan_init()
means the ~91 MB of registration tables are built once and shared
copy-on-write.

3. The division
---------------

Packets are grouped by the unordered pair of IP addresses. Not by the
5-tuple: IP fragments carry no ports, so a 5-tuple key would put a
fragmented datagram in a different worker from the flow it belongs to.
Everything that dissection accumulates - conversations, TCP sequence
analysis, reassembly - is then confined to one worker.

Pairs are assigned longest-processing-time-first: biggest pair to the
worker with the least work so far. Hashing the key instead leaves the
busiest worker 24% above ideal at 4 ways on real traffic and 72% at 16,
because flow sizes are nothing like uniform; the greedy assignment stays
within 1% up to 16 ways. That is why there is an index pass at all: the
assignment needs the counts.

Ties break on the key, so every process that plans the same capture gets
the same plan - the workers never compare notes.

Packets with no IP addresses - ARP, STP, and encapsulations the planner
does not parse - go to worker 0. On real traffic that is a few percent.

--shard-plan prints the division without dissecting anything, which is how
to tell whether a capture can be divided at all.

4. The workers
--------------

The parent builds the plan, creates two unlinked spool files per worker
and forks. Each worker reads the whole file, dissects and prints only the
packets the plan gives it into its output spool, and records (frame
number, offset, length) for each packet in its index spool. The parent
writes the preamble, walks the indexes in frame number order copying
bytes out of the spools, and writes the finale.

So the merge knows nothing about output formats: it moves the bytes a
packet produced, whatever they are.

A packet another worker owns still advances the state later packets are
printed relative to, so a worker keeps the frame numbering, the time
reference, the previous frame and the cumulative byte count up to date
for packets it never dissects (jobs_skip_packet() in tshark.c). Without
that, every time column would be measured from each worker's own first
packet.

The parent reaps the workers as they finish rather than in order. A
worker that stops on a bad file has printed the packets it got through,
exactly as a single process would have, so its exit status is passed on
and its output is still merged. One that is killed is different: with
--spill-dir it may have been holding the lock on the store they all
allocate from, and a lock whose owner is gone is never released, so the
parent kills the rest of the pass and fails the run rather than leaving
the survivors waiting on it for ever.

5. Numbering things in the order they are first seen
----------------------------------------------------

tcp.stream, udp.stream, ip.stream, eth.stream, conversation indices: all
of these are counters incremented as the dissection meets something new,
and a worker that only sees its own flows would start each of them at
zero. epan/ordinal.[ch] turns them from a static into a counter that can
be answered by a provider; with no provider installed, which is every
ordinary run, ordinal_next() is exactly the static it replaced.

When the output can show such a field - anything other than summary
columns - the workers run twice. The first pass dissects without
printing and records, in creation order, the frame each value was handed
out for. The parent merges those records by frame number, which is the
order one process would have created them in, and gives each worker back
the values it should hand out. The second pass prints.

Two things make that exact rather than approximate. A value can be asked
for with an identity - the pair of addresses, or addresses and ports -
for things that more than one worker can meet: the inner header of an
ICMP error names a flow that belongs to someone else, and a pair of MAC
addresses carries many flows. Everything numbering the same identity
gets the same value. And the second pass checks the frame of every value
it is handed against the frame the first pass recorded, so a dissection
that took a different path is a loud failure rather than quietly
different output.

The cost is that dissection happens twice, so this is skipped for
summary output.

6. Statistics that fold
-----------------------

A tap accumulates, and a worker's accumulator covers the flows it was
given. For the conversation and endpoint tables (-z conv,<type> and -z
endpoints,<type>) the accumulator is a list of rows - two addresses, two
ports, frames and bytes each way, first and last time - and rows add up.

So each worker, instead of printing its table, writes its rows to a
spool, and the parent adds together the rows that are the same
conversation: same type, same address pair, same ports. Folding, not
deduplicating. A conversation can legitimately be in two workers' tables,
because the inner header of an ICMP error names a flow that belongs to
someone else, and then each of them has counted part of it. A worker that
met the conversation from the other end has its own idea of which
direction is which, so the fold keys on the pair in a fixed order and
turns the counters round to match.

The parent then creates the same tables the -z arguments asked for -
which is the same code any other run creates them with - fills them with
the folded rows and prints them through the same draw functions. The
formatting, the sort order and the totals cannot drift from a serial run
because they are not reimplemented.

The draw functions print rows largest first and break ties by the order
the rows were created, so the order has to be the capture's rather than
each worker's. Every worker records the frame that created each of its
rows; no two workers share a frame, so sorting the folded rows by that
frame is the order one process would have built them in, and a row two
workers created is the earlier of the two - which is where a single
process would have created it, and the direction it would have had.

What is not folded is the conversation id, which is each worker's own
numbering of its own flows, and the dissector info block, which is a
per-dissector static that only the filter names the GUI builds come out
of. Neither reaches the printed table.

Network name resolution (-N n) is refused along with -z: a name a worker
learned from a DNS response in the capture belongs to that worker, and
the parent that prints the table never dissected anything.

7. What it refuses
------------------

Only cases where the output would match a single-process run are
accepted. The rest are refused rather than approximated:

    -z, other than      the other tap accumulators cover one worker's
    conv, endpoints     flows, and folding each of them back together
                        is work only that tap can do.
    -T json, jsonraw    the JSON writer puts the separators between
                        elements in as it goes, so each worker's spool
                        starts a fresh array.
    -R                  A read filter drops packets before they are
                        numbered, so the frame numbers would be each
                        worker's own.
    -w, --export-pdu    There would be nothing to merge in frame order.
    -q                  Likewise, unless -z asks for a table that folds,
                        which is then the whole output.
    Windows             No fork().

Two-pass analysis (-2) is supported. Each worker indexes every packet,
so that the frame numbering and the frames the second pass walks are the
capture's, but dissects only its own; the frames it does not own still
advance what later packets are printed relative to. The reference frame
is deliberately left for the second pass to find, since the first one
only saw this worker's packets.

A display filter (-Y) is supported, and costs the first pass: the
workers record which of their frames passed, the parent merges those
verdicts, and every worker gets the whole capture's set back. Without
that, a column measured from the previous displayed packet would be
measured from the previous packet this worker displayed.

8. What it cannot refuse
------------------------

Dissection state keyed on something other than the addresses in the
outer headers can still differ. Of the 254 captures in test/captures,
252 are byte-identical under -V -x at 3 workers, and the two that are
not show the two shapes of this:

  - sip-rtp.pcapng: RTP is announced by SDP carried on a different
    address pair, so the worker that owns the media never saw the
    announcement and dissects it as UDP.
  - uet-generated-crc-encaps.pcap: a tunnel, where one inner flow spans
    several outer address pairs, so its per-conversation packet count
    and timings are split between workers.

The same shape of hazard exists for TLS session resumption through the
global key map and for names learned from DNS. Nothing in a plan built
before dissection can know about those relationships, so --jobs says on
stderr that it dissects flows independently rather than quietly
differing.

9. Cost
-------

A real 526 MB capture, 749 804 packets, 669 address pairs, summary output:

    workers    wall    note
    1          4.25 s
    2          3.31 s
    4          2.55 s
    8          2.61 s  no further gain

The ceiling is the biggest flow: one conversation here is 36% of the
packets, so no division can beat 2.75x, and --shard-plan says so before
the run. Each worker also reads the whole file, and the index pass reads
it once more.

With everything spilled to disk (--spill-dir, --spill-after 0) the same
capture takes 4.19 s serially and 2.57 s with 4 workers, output
identical to an unspilled run in both cases: sharding and spilling help
each other, because the state is divided along with the packets and a
smaller arena is a warmer one.

Packet details (-V), where the output is 4 GB rather than 60 MB and
every packet costs two dissections because the numbering needs a first
pass:

    workers    wall
    1         22.14 s
    2         18.28 s
    4         14.26 s
    8         17.01 s

Less than the summary case gains, and for a reason worth knowing: what
a worker spends its time on here is formatting, and the output has to
come out in frame order. Each worker prints into a spool of its own and
the parent copies the spools out in order afterwards, which lets a
worker print a long run of the capture without waiting for anything.
Two alternatives were tried and both came out slower: sending the
bytes to the parent as they are printed (19.5 s against 12.7 s on the
same machine, because a worker then waits whenever its channel fills
and the output is elsewhere), and keeping the spools but streaming the
records so the copying overlaps the printing (13.9 s against 13.0 s).
Ordered output wants unbounded room to run ahead in, and a file is the
cheapest way to have it.

The measurements above are macOS on an Apple M4. The same capture on
Ubuntu 26.04, aarch64, eight cores, gives the same shape: summary
output 4.17 s against 2.52 s with four workers, packet details 20.01 s
against 12.73 s, everything spilled 5.45 s against 3.91 s, and the same
252 of 254 captures byte-identical under --jobs 3. The two that differ
are the two listed in section 8.

The store is created before the fork and every worker allocates from
it, so --spill-after is a watermark for the run and not for each
worker, which matters because the worker that gets the largest
conversation needs the most memory. At --spill-after 512M, one worker
and four workers both peak at about 508 MB in RAM (4.08 s and 2.68 s).

10. Limits and next steps
-------------------------

More taps. The conversation and endpoint tables fold because their
accumulator is a list of rows; the rest do not, and each of them is its
own piece of work (stats_tree burst rates, io_graph frame-number
tie-breaks). What the two that do fold have in common is a table the
parent can be handed, not a flag on the tap.

JSON. The workers would have to emit elements that can be concatenated,
which means the JSON writer putting its separators somewhere other than
between the elements as it goes.

The second pass. Output that shows an ordinal costs two dissections.
The first one could be cheaper - it needs the conversations, not the
fields - but nothing in epan distinguishes those today.

Affinity. The answer to section 8 is to let a worker that discovers a
relationship - SDP announcing a media address, a tunnel carrying a flow
that belongs elsewhere - hand that flow to the worker that owns the
other end, which means the plan has to be adjustable while the run is
in progress.

11. Reproducing the measurements
--------------------------------

test/benchmark/gen_pcap.py generates captures with an exact packet and
flow count; test/benchmark/flow_split.py splits a real capture into
flow-disjoint files, which is what the measurements that predate --jobs
used. See test/benchmark/README.md.
